upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about Thomas Malthus's population theory, per Goh Cheng Leong, is NOT correct?","o":["Malthus's pessimistic prediction has been fully disproved and holds no relevance today, since more than half the human race is now well-nourished","Malthus, a British clergyman and political economist (1766-1834), warned that population increasing in geometrical progression would eventually outstrip food production","Three developments Malthus did not foresee — technological/industrial advances, agricultural mechanization, and family planning — have modified his pessimistic outlook","Despite these mitigating developments, more than half the human race remains malnourished, so Malthus's predictions may not yet be fully disproved"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect and reverses the text's actual caution — the text explicitly states that since more than half the human race is malnourished, there is 'no guarantee that Malthus' predictions may not yet take place,' not that his theory has been fully disproved. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about world population growth, per Goh Cheng Leong's 1977-era data:\n1. The world's population had almost trebled in the preceding 70 years.\n2. The United Nations estimated an average annual increase of about 1.5%, projecting roughly 6.65 billion people by 2000 A.D.\n3. Between the Second World War and 1952, world food production grew faster than world population.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect and reverses the actual figures — the text states world population increased by 12% in that period while food production increased by only 9%, meaning population outpaced food production, not the reverse. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

# ==================================================================
# SECTION 2: FACTORS INFLUENCING DISTRIBUTION
# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"Match List-I (Population Density Factor) with List-II (Explanation, per Goh Cheng Leong) and select the correct answer using the code below.\nList-I:\nA. Climate\nB. Physical relief\nC. Soils\nD. Mineral resources\nList-II:\n1. Lowlands and river valleys support the most people due to ease of communication and cultivation\n2. Volcanic, alluvial, loess and glacial soils support dense populations regardless of local climate\n3. Probably the single greatest deciding factor, shaping vegetation, crops and overall habitability\n4. Commercially exploitable metallic ores or fuels support industry, which in turn supports high density\nCodes:","o":["A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4","A-1, B-3, C-2, D-4","A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4","A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2"],"a":0,"e":"The correct match is A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4: climate is described as probably the greatest deciding factor; physical relief favours lowlands/valleys; certain soils (volcanic, alluvial, loess, glacial) support dense population independent of climate; and mineral resources support industry-driven density."})

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about overpopulation, as defined by Goh Cheng Leong, is NOT correct?","o":["Overpopulation is an absolute measure based solely on the number of people per square kilometre, regardless of resources or technology","Overpopulation is a relative term referring to an area where natural resources and technical competence can no longer comfortably support the inhabitants","A few nomads per square kilometre in the arid Middle East may be considered overpopulated","192 persons per square kilometre in the industrial Midlands of the United Kingdom may not be considered overpopulated"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect and reverses the text's explicit definition — overpopulation is described as a RELATIVE term (dependent on resources and technical competence), not an absolute density figure; this is precisely why a sparse desert population can be 'overpopulated' while a dense industrial one is not. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about the pattern of world population distribution, per Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. Agricultural Monsoon Asia forms the largest area of continuous high population density, including China, Japan, India, Bangladesh, Sri Lanka and Indonesia.\n2. The Nile valley and delta has been a region of unusually high density since the establishment of Egyptian civilization, aided by basin irrigation and, more recently, multi-purpose dams like Aswan.\n3. The taiga of Eurasia and Canadian coniferous forests are classified among the very densely populated parts of the world.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the text classifies the cool temperate forests, including the taiga of Eurasia and Canadian coniferous forests, as only MODERATELY populated, not very densely populated. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

