# SECTION 3: NORTH-WEST EUROPE VS AMAZON BASIN COMPARISON
# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"Assertion (A): North-West Europe supports a very high population density (over 77 persons per sq km) despite its small size, while the Amazon basin remains very sparsely populated (below 4 persons per sq km) despite its vast area.\nReason (R): North-West Europe's temperate climate is stimulating and conducive to sustained work, whereas the Amazon's hot, humid, enervating climate makes prolonged labour difficult.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above Assertion (A) and Reason (R)?","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true and R explains A — Goh Cheng Leong explicitly contrasts North-West Europe's stimulating temperate climate (favourable for sustained work and development) with the Amazon's oppressive, enervating heat and humidity as a key reason for their starkly different population densities, alongside other factors like industrialization and transport."})

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about the Amazon basin, per Goh Cheng Leong's comparative study, is NOT correct?","o":["Despite adverse conditions for large-scale rubber cultivation, the Amazon basin accounts for over 25 per cent of the world's natural rubber production","The Amazonian natives, such as the Boros, remain at a Stone Age level of civilization with no permanent settlements, similar to the Pygmies of the Zaire basin","Lumbering in the Amazon is difficult because its wide variety of tree species do not occur in pure stands","The Amazon's heavily leached, acidic soil deteriorates rapidly once cleared of forest, limiting its capacity to support large settled populations"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect — the text explicitly states the Amazon accounts for LESS than 1 per cent of the world's natural rubber production, not over 25 per cent, despite being the natural home of rubber, due to adverse climatic, soil and environmental conditions for large-scale cultivation. The other three statements are correct."})

