upsc.append({"q":"According to Goh Cheng Leong, how did Japan address her rapid population growth, and what was the outcome?","o":["Japan practised family planning, cutting her annual birth rate from about 22 per thousand in 1947 to about 13 per thousand, alongside a rise in living standards","Japan encouraged large-scale emigration to Brazil and the U.S.A. as her primary population-control strategy","Japan relied mainly on increasing food imports rather than reducing her birth rate","Japan's population growth was addressed chiefly through compulsory land redistribution programmes"],"a":0,"e":"The text explicitly credits Japan's success to family planning/birth control, which nearly halved her birth rate (from 22 to 13 per thousand) within two decades, accompanied by a rise in living standards — not emigration, food imports, or land redistribution as the primary solution."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about relieving world population problems, per Goh Cheng Leong's three main approaches:\n1. Reducing the rate of population increase through family planning is one recognised approach.\n2. Increasing food yields via new agricultural techniques, drought/cold-resistant crop varieties, and reclaiming previously 'waste' lands is a second approach.\n3. Migration to underpopulated regions like Australia, Canada and parts of the U.S.S.R. can, by itself, reduce the total world population.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the text explicitly states migration 'may not reduce the total population of the world,' though it helps relieve regional overcrowding by redistributing people to underpopulated areas. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

