upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about India's population distribution and density, per NCERT — entirely new content not found in Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. Uttar Pradesh, Maharashtra, Bihar, West Bengal, Andhra Pradesh, Tamil Nadu, Madhya Pradesh, Rajasthan, Karnataka and Gujarat together account for about 76% of India's total population.\n2. India's population density (2001) of 313 persons per sq km ranked it the most densely populated country in Asia, ahead of both Bangladesh and Japan.\n3. Population density in India ranges from as low as 13 persons per sq km in Arunachal Pradesh to 9,340 persons per sq km in the National Capital Territory of Delhi.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 2 is incorrect — NCERT states India's density (313/sq km, 2001) ranked only THIRD in Asia, behind Bangladesh (849) and Japan (334), not first. Statements 1 and 3 are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Match List-I (Phase of India's Population Growth, per NCERT) with List-II (Defining Characteristic) and select the correct answer using the code below.\nList-I:\nA. Phase I (1901-1921)\nB. Phase II (1921-1951)\nC. Phase III (1951-1981)\nD. Phase IV (post-1981)\nList-II:\n1. 'Population explosion' — rapid mortality decline with continued high fertility, average annual growth of 2.2%\n2. 'Stagnant/stationary phase' — high birth and death rates, even negative growth in 1911-1921\n3. Growth remains high but gradually slows, linked to rising age at marriage and female education\n4. 'Steady population growth' — improved health/sanitation lowers mortality while birth rate stays high\nCodes:","o":["A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3","A-2, B-1, C-4, D-3","A-2, B-4, C-3, D-1","A-4, B-2, C-1, D-3"],"a":0,"e":"The correct match is A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3: Phase I was the stagnant phase with high birth/death rates; Phase II saw steady growth as mortality fell; Phase III was the population explosion (2.2% average annual growth); and Phase IV shows gradually slowing growth linked to later marriage age and female education."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about density measures used by NCERT for India — concepts entirely absent from Goh Cheng Leong's treatment:\n1. Physiological density is calculated as total population divided by net cultivated area.\n2. Agricultural density is calculated as total agricultural population (cultivators, agricultural labourers and their families) divided by net cultivable area.\n3. These measures are considered cruder indicators of human-land pressure than simple crude population density.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect and reverses NCERT's actual point — physiological and agricultural density are described as giving a BETTER insight into human-land pressure than the cruder measure of simple crude population density, not a cruder one. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Assertion (A): India's Phase III of population growth (1951-1981), the 'population explosion,' saw the country's highest average annual growth rate of the twentieth century.\nReason (R): This period combined a rapid decline in mortality with continued high fertility, alongside post-Independence developmental planning and increased international in-migration from neighbouring countries.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above Assertion (A) and Reason (R)?","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true and R explains A — NCERT attributes Phase III's roughly 2.2% average annual growth rate (India's highest sustained rate) precisely to the combination of falling mortality, persistently high fertility, post-Independence planning improving living conditions, and added migration inflows from Tibet, Bangladesh, Nepal and Pakistan."})

